{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-73340","model_call":32,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"h.r.3162\" \"hr 3162\" \"h r 3162\" \"usa patriot\") (+ \"357-66\" \"357–66\" \"357 - 66\") (+ \"98-1\" \"98–1\" \"98 - 1\"))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"h.r.3162\" \"hr 3162\" \"h r 3162\" \"usa patriot\") (+ \"357-66\" \"357–66\" \"357 - 66\") (+ \"98-1\" \"98–1\" \"98 - 1\"))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_17747056#1_34930249","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q800","rank":1,"snippet":"Bush: signing of USA PATRIOT Act Pres. George W. Bush signing the USA PATRIOT Act in the East Room of the White House, Washington, D.C., October 26, 2001. George W. Bush Library/NARA History In the wake of the September 11 attacks, the Bush administration submitted to Congress draft legislation designed to expand the powers of the government to surveil, investigate, and detain suspected terrorists. The eventual Senate bill, the Uniting and Strengthening America (USA) Act, was passed (96–1) on October 11. The House measure, which included much of the text of the Senate’s USA Act, was passed (337–79) on October 12 as the Uniting and Strengthening America (USA) Act. On October 23 the House introduced a compromise bill, the <cover>USA PATRIOT Act, which was passed (357–66) the following day. The Senate adopted (98–1) </cover>the House bill without amendment on October 25, and the following day Bush signed the measure into law.\" , \"start_char\": 1024.000000 , \"title\": \"USA PATRIOT Act | Facts, History, Acronym, & Controversy | Britannica\" , \"url\": \"https://www.britannica.com/topic/USA-PATRIOT-Act\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_17747056#1_34930249","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":221},"model_calls":32,"document_judgments":23,"relevance_3":16,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-73340","model_call":32,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q800"},"model_calls":32,"document_judgments":23,"relevance_3":16,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-73340","model_call":32,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q800"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q800"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_17747056#2_34931921","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q800","rank":2,"snippet":"Library/NARA History In the wake of the September 11 attacks, the Bush administration submitted to Congress draft legislation designed to expand the powers of the government to surveil, investigate, and detain suspected terrorists. The eventual Senate bill, the Uniting and Strengthening America (USA) Act, was passed (96–1) on October 11. The House measure, which included much of the text of the Senate’s USA Act, was passed (337–79) on October 12 as the Uniting and Strengthening America (USA) Act. On October 23 the House introduced a compromise bill, the <cover>USA PATRIOT Act, which was passed (357–66) the following day. The Senate adopted (98–1) </cover>the House bill without amendment on October 25, and the following day Bush signed the measure into law. Provisions Some key provisions of the legislation consisted of amendments to the Wiretap Act (1968; amended 1986 and 1994), which had prohibited eavesdropping by the government on private face-to-face, telephone, and electronic communications except as authorized by court order in narrowly defined circumstances in cases of serious crimes. Sections 201 and 202 of the USA PATRIOT Act added computer and terrorist crimes to the list of serious offenses in connection with which ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_17747056#2_34931921","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":8},"model_calls":32,"document_judgments":23,"relevance_3":16,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-73340","model_call":32,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q800"},"model_calls":32,"document_judgments":23,"relevance_3":16,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-73340","model_call":32,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q800"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q800"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1393034455#0_2889021612","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q800","rank":3,"snippet":"a key part - and often the leading role - in a number of successful operations to protect innocent Americans from the deadly plans of terrorists dedicated to destroying America and our way of life. While the results have been important, in passing the Patriot Act, Congress provided for only modest, incremental changes in the law. Congress simply took existing legal principles and retrofitted them to preserve the lives and liberty of the American people from the challenges posed by a global terrorist network. PDF version The USA PATRIOT Act: Preserving Life and Liberty (Uniting and Strengthening America by Providing Appropriate Tools Required to Intercept and Obstruct Terrorism) Congress enacted the Patriot Act by overwhelming, bipartisan margins, arming law enforcement with new tools to detect and prevent terrorism: The <cover>USA Patriot Act was passed nearly unanimously by the Senate 98-1, and 357-66 </cover>in the House, with the support of members from across the political spectrum. The Act Improves Our Counter-Terrorism Efforts in Several Significant Ways:\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"\" , \"url\": \"https://www.justice.gov/archive/ll/highlights.htm\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1393034455#0_2889021612","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":10},"model_calls":32,"document_judgments":23,"relevance_3":16,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
