{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-73340","model_call":67,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q820"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q820"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_1778472344#3_2688093350","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q820","rank":2,"snippet":"06_1778472344#3_2688093350\" , \"end_char\": 3354.000000 , \"headings\": \"How to Win the War on Terror: Repeal the Patriot Act How to Win the War on Terror: Repeal the Patriot Act MORE: \" , \"segment\": \"After the passage of the Patriot Act in 2001, our rights to privacy as citizens were signed away. Sen. Russ Feingold (D-WI) was the only senator who <cover>voted no to the bill that gave massive new powers to the DOJ. Sen. Mary Landrieu (D-LA) didn’t vote yes or no. The House vote for the 2001 bill was also pretty one-sided - the Patriot Act passed 357-</cover>66. When it came up for reauthorization in 2006, the Senate passed it 89-10 (there were several who voted yes in 2001 and no in 2006) and the house passed it 280-138. And in 2011, the Patriot Act was extended through 2015 on an 86-12 vote in the Senate, and a 275-144 vote in the House. You can see how your members of Congress voted here, here and here. And it’s important to note that ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_1778472344#3_2688093350","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":9},"model_calls":67,"document_judgments":40,"relevance_3":18,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
