{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-73340","model_call":70,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q823"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q823"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1431644496#8_2749707629","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q823","rank":4,"snippet":"would add a new sunset date of December 31, 2013, for National Security Letter authorities, which were dramatically expanded in the USA PATRIOT Act. In addition, Sen. Leahy’s proposal would require the FBI to retain an internal statement of facts demonstrating the relevancy of information sought to its investigation before it can issue a National Security Letter (NSL). The Leahy bill also would require the attorney general and the Justice Department inspector general to provide periodic reports to congressional overseers to ensure that certain investigative tools are being used responsibly. [ S.290; Leahy Press Release, <cover>1/26/11; Washington Post, 2/9/11] Sen. McConnell – S. 291 Senate Republicans introduced S. 291, which would make the expiring provisions of permanent. Key Roll Call Votes: On February 27, 2010, President Obama signed H.R. 3961 (Public Law 111-141 ), a one-year extension of several expiring provisions of the Patriot Act. </cover>Three provisions were extended:\" , \"start_char\": 5901.000000 , \"title\": \"H.R. 514, PATRIOT Act Extension\" , \"url\": \"http://www.dpc.senate.gov/dpcdoc.cfm?doc_name=lb-112-1-7\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1431644496#13_2749714403","rank":1,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1431644496#14_2749715819","rank":2,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_1431644496#15_2749717303","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":5},"model_calls":70,"document_judgments":41,"relevance_3":18,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
