{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-73340","model_call":71,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"patriot act\" (+ aclu \"american civil liberties union\" eff \"electronic frontier\") (+ \"357-66\" \"357 - 66\" \"98-1\" \"98 - 1\" feingold))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"patriot act\" (+ aclu \"american civil liberties union\" eff \"electronic frontier\") (+ \"357-66\" \"357 - 66\" \"98-1\" \"98 - 1\" feingold))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_254841671#0_470248982","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q824","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_254841671#0_470248982\" , \"end_char\": 1486.000000 , \"headings\": \"A Breakdown of the JUSTICE Act A Breakdown of the JUSTICE Act\" , \"segment\": \"A Breakdown of the JUSTICE Act | <cover>American Civil Liberties Union A Breakdown of the JUSTICE Act Senator Russ Feingold and nine other senators introduced S. 1686, the JUSTICE Act in the 111th Congress, which reforms our surveillance laws, including the Patriot Act </cover>and the FISA Amendments Act of 2008, an even broader expansion of government power. The bill reins in the government’s spying powers by: Protecting the privacy of records. The JUSTICE Act amends the national security letter (NSL) authority so that the government can only access communications, financial and credit records when they have something to do with a terror suspect or spy. Under the original Patriot Act, the government can collect the records of innocent people whenever it deems them “relevant” to an investigation – without any oversight by an impartial court. The current standard is so low that independent audits found that approximately 50,000 are issued "},"time":446},"model_calls":71,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":18,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
