{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-73340","model_call":76,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"usa patriot act\" (+ \"congressional research service\" crs \"legislative history\") (+ \"357\" \"66\" \"98\" \"1\"))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"usa patriot act\" (+ \"congressional research service\" crs \"legislative history\") (+ \"357\" \"66\" \"98\" \"1\"))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_86150574#0_97405510","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q828","rank":1,"snippet":"1444.000000 , \"headings\": \"USA Act and the Financial Anti-Terrorism Act USA Act and the Financial Anti-Terrorism Act Contents USA Act Financial Anti-Terrorism Act Legislative history: combining into the USA PATRIOT Act External links \" , \"segment\": \"USA Act and the Financial Anti-Terrorism Act - Wikipedia USA Act and the Financial Anti-Terrorism Act From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to navigation Jump to search The USA Act and the Financial Anti-Terrorism Act are the legislative precursors to the <cover>USA PATRIOT Act . Contents 1 USA Act 2 Financial Anti-Terrorism Act 3 Legislative history: </cover>combining into the USA PATRIOT Act 4 External links USA Act The USA Act ( Uniting and Strengthening America Act of 2001) is an expansion of the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act (FISA) of 1978. The primary difference between the USA Act and FISA is the definition of terrorism. In FISA, terrorism is limited to acts that are \"backed by a foreign power.\" The phrase \"foreign power\" is commonly interpreted to refer to a foreign government. Focus on Al Qaeda after September 11 raised the issue that there are terrorists who are not backed by "},"time":532},"model_calls":76,"document_judgments":46,"relevance_3":18,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
